AWS CLOUD AND DEVOPS SCENARIO-BASED ASSESSMENT
AWS Cloud | Terraform | Git | Jenkins | SonarQube | Docker | Kubernetes

INSTRUCTIONS
- Total questions: 30 | Maximum marks: 100
- Question types: Single Select (SS), Multiple Select (MS), and Negative Select (NS).
- For MS questions, select all correct options. Unless your assessment platform applies partial scoring, award marks only when all correct choices and no incorrect choices are selected.
- For NS questions, select the option that should NOT be followed or is least appropriate.
- Recommended duration: 75-90 minutes.
------------------------------------------------------------
Q1. An application runs on two Amazon EC2 instances in one availability zone. A zonal outage makes it unavailable. Which changes improve resilience?
Type: MS | Marks: 3
A. Deploy instances across multiple availability zones
B. Place the instances behind a Application Load Balancer
C. Use automatic scaling and health-based replacement
D. Increase only the operating-system disk size
E. Configure health checks

Q2. Several engineers manage the same Amazon cloud environment using Terraform. Local state files are creating conflicting updates. What is the best solution?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Commit the state file to Git
B. Use a remote backend with access control and state locking
C. Keep the state on one engineer's laptop
D. Copy the state file through email after every change

Q3. Jenkins deploys infrastructure to the cloud. Which credential practice should NOT be used?
Type: NS | Marks: 3
A. Use a IAM role for service accounts / EKS Pod Identity or temporary credentials
B. Apply least-privilege permissions
C. Hard-code a long-lived secret in the Jenkinsfile
D. Store any unavoidable secret in an approved credential store

Q4. Compilation and unit tests pass, but the SonarQube Quality Gate fails because of new critical issues. What should the pipeline do?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Continue to production because compilation passed
B. Stop promotion and require remediation
C. Delete the SonarQube project
D. Mark the scan as informational for all production branches

Q5. A Docker image is 1.8 GB and takes too long to build and deploy. Which actions should reduce its size?
Type: MS | Marks: 3
A. Use a multi-stage build
B. Use a minimal runtime base image
C. Add compilers and debugging tools to the final image
D. Use .dockerignore
E. Remove build caches and unnecessary packages

Q6. A newly deployed pod remains in Pending state. Which checks are relevant?
Type: MS | Marks: 3
A. Review pod events with kubectl describe
B. Check node CPU and memory capacity
C. Review node selectors, affinity, taints, and tolerations
D. Rebuild the Git repository
E. Check PersistentVolumeClaim binding

Q7. Production needs an urgent fix while the development branch contains incomplete features. What is the safest Git approach?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Deploy the development branch
B. Create a hotfix from the protected production baseline
C. Edit files directly on the production server
D. Force-push development to main

Q8. A Kubernetes workload must read objects from one Amazon S3 bucket. What is the most secure authentication design?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Store administrator keys in a ConfigMap
B. Use a dedicated IAM role for service accounts / EKS Pod Identity with least-privilege bucket access
C. Bake access keys into the Docker image
D. Make the bucket public

Q9. A firewall or security-group rule was changed manually, creating Terraform drift. Which action should NOT normally be performed?
Type: NS | Marks: 3
A. Run terraform plan to identify drift
B. Decide whether the manual change is approved
C. Represent the intended configuration in code and apply it through the workflow
D. Edit the Terraform state file manually without review

Q10. Jenkins checks out the code, but docker build reports that a required file is missing from the build context. What should be checked first?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Kubernetes replica count
B. Dockerfile paths, build context, and .dockerignore
C. Cloud autoscaling settings
D. SonarQube user permissions

Q11. A Kubernetes pod reports ImagePullBackOff while pulling from the Amazon ECR. Which checks are appropriate?
Type: MS | Marks: 3
A. Verify the image repository and tag
B. Verify registry authorization
C. Review pod events
D. Check registry network and DNS connectivity
E. Change the Git branching model

Q12. Two engineers modify the same Kubernetes Deployment manifest and Git reports a merge conflict. What should be done?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Force-push one developer's branch
B. Review both intended changes, resolve the conflict, validate the manifest, and commit
C. Delete the repository history
D. Modify only the live cluster and ignore Git

Q13. Which practice should NOT be used for production Terraform deployments?
Type: NS | Marks: 3
A. Review the saved plan
B. Restrict apply permissions
C. Automatically approve every destructive plan
D. Version-control Terraform configuration

Q14. A pod enters CrashLoopBackOff after deployment. Which initial checks are valid?
Type: MS | Marks: 3
A. Review current and previous container logs
B. Review pod events
C. Check commands, environment variables, and mounted configuration
D. Check readiness and liveness probes
E. Check resource limits and termination reason

Q15. The team must trace a production container image back to the exact source revision. Which tagging strategy is best?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Use only latest
B. Use an immutable tag such as Git commit SHA or release ID
C. Use the developer's first name
D. Retag the image manually after deployment

Q16. Which controls reduce accidental changes to the production branch?
Type: MS | Marks: 3
A. Protected branch rules
B. Pull-request review
C. Automated tests
D. Required Quality Gate
E. Unrestricted force-push

Q17. Unit tests execute in Jenkins, but SonarQube shows zero code coverage. What is the best next action?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Increase cluster size
B. Generate the supported coverage report and configure the scanner with its correct path
C. Delete the .git directory
D. Increase application replicas

Q18. Which secret-management practice is least appropriate?
Type: NS | Marks: 3
A. Restrict access using RBAC or IAM
B. Use an approved secret-management service
C. Commit plaintext production passwords to Git
D. Rotate compromised credentials

Q19. Development, test, and production require similar networks with different CIDR ranges and sizes. What is the preferred Terraform design?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Maintain three unrelated manual copies
B. Use a reusable module with environment-specific validated inputs
C. Create production manually in the console
D. Store network values in the Dockerfile

Q20. Build results vary because Jenkins agents have different Terraform and Docker versions and retain files from previous jobs. What is the best correction?
Type: SS | Marks: 3
A. Standardize tool versions and use clean, preferably ephemeral agents
B. Run every job permanently on the Jenkins controller
C. Remove all version constraints
D. Replace Jenkins with manual execution

Q21. A new Kubernetes release must be deployed with minimal disruption. Which controls are appropriate?
Type: MS | Marks: 4
A. Readiness probes
B. A controlled rolling or canary strategy
C. Health and error monitoring during rollout
D. Rollback to a known-good immutable version
E. Delete all old pods before creating replacements

Q22. Terraform plan unexpectedly proposes replacement of a critical production database. What should NOT be done?
Type: NS | Marks: 4
A. Stop before apply
B. Review state, configuration, and provider changes
C. Assess backup and recovery implications
D. Apply immediately because Terraform generated the plan

Q23. A container works locally but fails in the Amazon EKS. Which differences should be investigated?
Type: MS | Marks: 4
A. Environment-specific configuration
B. Container and Service ports
C. Secrets and ConfigMaps
D. External service dependencies
E. Readiness and liveness probes

Q24. A Jenkins cloud platform credential is suspected to be compromised. Which actions are required?
Type: MS | Marks: 4
A. Revoke or rotate it
B. Review audit and access logs
C. Determine resources and permissions exposed
D. Replace it with a least-privilege identity
E. Continue using it until the next release

Q25. After a release, pods show Running but users receive HTTP 503. Which checks are relevant?
Type: MS | Marks: 4
A. Readiness status
B. Service selectors and endpoints
C. Ingress or load-balancer backend health
D. Application logs and listening port
E. Git repository storage

Q26. A cloud platform credential was accidentally committed to Git. Which actions should be taken?
Type: MS | Marks: 4
A. Revoke or rotate it immediately
B. Remove it from current code and relevant history
C. Review logs for possible unauthorized use
D. Enable secret scanning or preventive controls
E. Only delete the visible line and keep using the credential

Q27. Which controls strengthen a production CI/CD pipeline?
Type: MS | Marks: 4
A. Automated unit and integration tests
B. A blocking SonarQube Quality Gate
C. Container image scanning
D. Reviewed Terraform plan
E. Controlled production approval and post-deployment validation

Q28. A container repeatedly terminates with an out-of-memory reason. What is the best next action?
Type: SS | Marks: 4
A. Increase the Git timeout
B. Review actual memory usage and tune application behavior, requests, and limits
C. Disable SonarQube
D. Delete Terraform state

Q29. Which container tagging strategy should NOT be relied upon for production traceability?
Type: NS | Marks: 4
A. Immutable semantic version
B. Git commit SHA
C. Unique build or release identifier
D. Only the mutable latest tag

Q30. An end-to-end pipeline follows Git to Jenkins to SonarQube to a container registry to managed Kubernetes. Which stages are appropriate release gates?
Type: MS | Marks: 4
A. Controlled checkout from version control
B. Build and automated tests
C. Static analysis and Quality Gate
D. Publish a versioned and scanned image
E. Controlled deployment, health validation, and rollback readiness



